1. Structural Overview of the Diagram
The diagram consists of two main pools (with the second pool divided into two lanes), connected by sequence flows (solid arrows, showing order of work within a lane/pool) and message flows (dashed arrows, showing communication or data exchange between pools):

Top Pool (1 Lane): Represents the primary operational/user-facing or monitoring workflow. It starts with a Start Event, moves through sequential tasks, splits at an Exclusive Gateway (X) into three alternative/branching paths, merges back at an X gateway, continues through further tasks and message exchanges, and finishes at an End Event.

Middle Pool — Upper Lane: Receives initial and follow-up messages from the top pool, makes decisions at two X gateways, sends a message back up to the top pool, routes one branch into the lower lane, and merges into a final task that sends a message up to the top pool.

Middle Pool — Lower Lane: Handles a three-step sub-process triggered from the upper lane's lower branch and feeds its output back up into the upper lane's final task.
